    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1600-0889.2010.00494.x",
    "abstract": "We have been conducting continuous measurements of Methane (sCH4) concentration from an expanding network of towers (JR-STATION: Japan\u2013Russia Siberian Tall Tower Inland Observation Network) located in taiga, steppe and wetland biomes of Siberia since 2004. High daytime means (>2000 ppb) observed simultaneously at several towers during winter, together with in situ weather data and NCEP/NCAR reanalysis data, indicate that high pressure systems caused CH4 accumulation at subcontinental scale due to the widespread formation of an inversion layer. Daytime means sometimes exceeded 2000 ppb, particularly in the summer of 2007 when temperature and precipitation rates were anomalously high over West Siberia, which implies that CH4 emission from wetlands were exceptionally high in 2007. Many hot spots detected by MODIS in the summer of 2007 illustrate that the contribution of biomass burning also cannot be neglected. Daytime mean CH4 concentrations from the Siberian tower sites were generally higher than CH4 values reported at NOAA coastal sites in the same latitudinal zone, and the difference in concentrations between two sets of sites was reproduced with a coupled Eulerian\u2013Lagrangian transport model. Simulations of emissions from different CH4 sources suggested that the major contributor to variation switched from wetlands during summer to fossil fuel during winter.",
    "field": "Atmospheric greenhouse gas measurement",
    "problem": "The scarcity of continuous, high-frequency methane measurements across Siberia's vast and heterogeneous landscapes has limited the ability to accurately quantify regional emission sources (e.g., wetlands, fossil fuels), understand their temporal variability, and identify the atmospheric conditions that drive significant concentration changes.",
    "method": "The authors use continuous, high-frequency methane measurements from a network of tall towers across Siberia, analyzed with meteorological data and a transport model, to identify emission sources and understand the factors controlling observed spatial and temporal concentration variations.",
    "task": "The task is to characterize the spatial and temporal variations of atmospheric methane over Siberia and identify its primary sources by analyzing continuous concentration measurements from a dedicated network of observation towers.",
